+# Movement 004 cold-read review
+
+**Read status:** Fresh-context **simulated cold read**, manuscript only. This is an editorial simulation, not a real audience measurement, demographic study, or sales forecast. I read all eight supplied chapters in order: chapters 23–30. I am judging only this movement, not the full book. No editorial canon evidence was supplied.
+
+## Unscored reader response
+
+Interest first caught in chapter 23 when the crew's honest “no” earns them a free cart ride. That converts an apparently failed mountain job into reputation, kindness, and forward motion without forcing the point. The boot exchange then gives the recovery journey warmth and personality. Interest intensified at the plank in chapter 24: the hidden hundred-mark contract, Pike's uncharacteristic stillness, and “quiet ground” create an immediate mystery with social proof that the danger is real. From chapter 27 onward, the measured approach to the Fallow Ring steadily turns inquiry into threat. The registry stake, Cael's undeclared exception, the missing instruments, and the den beneath the far lip form an excellent escalation chain.
+
+Interest slipped in two places. First, the early pages of chapter 23 load many injuries, dates, debts, place names, and prior incidents before this cold reader has a firm emotional handhold. The problem is mild because the cart offer arrives quickly. Second, the repeated cord-and-offset procedure in chapter 28 becomes more methodical than suspenseful for a stretch. More seriously, the literal `[[TIER-B: ...]]` notes in chapters 25 and 29 break the fiction completely; they read as production scaffolding rather than manuscript.
+
+Cael matters most. His habit of measuring everything is both competence and wound, and the movement makes his special condition morally consequential: he discovers that his borrowed fragments still function inside the Ring, hides that truth from his crew, then becomes responsible for leading five effectively unpowered people away from a stillhound den. Karis is a close second because her intellectual hunger supplies much of the movement's pleasure without erasing her fear.
+
+I expect the next event to be an attempted retreat across the exposed floor while the stillhounds return from the east. I also expect Cael to deploy one or more fragments openly, forcing the concealed fact into the crew's relationships. Yes, I would continue immediately. The chapter 30 ending converts a fascinating location into an urgent tactical and ethical problem.
+
+## Reader-lens scores
+
+These scores are editorial judgments. A 5 means understandable but inconsistent, 7 engaging with located weaknesses, and 9 compelling with few substantial distractions. The two lenses are not averaged.
+
+### Fluent thirteen-year-old reader
+
+| Dimension | Score | Exact passage location and reason | Confidence |
+|---|---:|---|---|
+| Opening pull | 7 | Ch. 23, opening inventory through “four who were not fit to”: concrete physical trouble and a transport problem create stakes, but the concentration of dates, injuries, names, and prior events delays attachment. The keeper's cart offer is the first strong emotional hook. | High |
+| Keep reading | 9 | Ch. 24, from Cael drawing the cream paper from beneath Pike's stone through the explanation of quiet ground; ch. 30, final far-lip reveal. Each local question pays off by exposing a larger one, and the final danger is immediate. | High |
+| Interest / freshness | 9 | Ch. 28, discovery of registry stake 31 on the invisible boundary; ch. 29, Path trials; ch. 30, the seamless floor and den. Bureaucracy, field science, magic suppression, and monster ecology combine in a distinctive way. | High |
+| Clarity / flow | 7 | Ch. 28, the sixty-two-cord circuit and repeated six-and-a-half-foot offsets are understandable but demanding; the two raw `[[TIER-B: ...]]` insertions in chs. 25 and 29 are unmistakable breaks. Scene geography at the Ring is otherwise unusually clear. | High |
+| Character attachment | 9 | Ch. 23, Brom waiting to the exact hour before using his wrist and then carrying all three boxes; ch. 29, Lira discovering that what remains is “Legs.” Recovery choices reveal pride, restraint, and vulnerability through action. | High |
+| Humor / warmth | 8 | Ch. 23, the oversized boots and Karis accounting for “a boot and a half”; ch. 29, Seln correcting the concealment count from two to three. The humor belongs to character and relieves pressure without puncturing it. | High |
+| Action / suspense | 8 | Chs. 27–30, approach, boundary test, controlled trials, entry, timed exploration, and den discovery. There is little combat in the supplied movement, but the contest against time, ignorance, and the Ring's rules is trackable and increasingly costly. | High |
+| Progression payoff | 9 | Ch. 23, Cael learns the mule's pace and Brom reclaims his wrist; ch. 26, Karis lights the lamp; ch. 29, each person discovers what skill remains without a Path. Effort changes capability and self-understanding visibly. | High |
+| Connection | 8 | Chs. 23–27 repeatedly carry the cart loss, rope debt, healing dates, board standing, letters, boots, maps, and prior promises forward. A cold reader can follow their present meaning, though the quantity of referenced history sometimes feels like arriving mid-conversation. | Medium-high |
+| Read-aloud quality | 7 | Ch. 27, the first approach to the boundary, and ch. 30, the den reveal, have strong cadence and clean referents. Several long analytical sentences require careful breath and the two production placeholders cannot be read as finished prose. | High |
+
+### Adult genre reader
+
+| Dimension | Score | Exact passage location and reason | Confidence |
+|---|---:|---|---|
+| Opening pull | 8 | Ch. 23, the injury-and-debt inventory followed by the keeper rewarding the crew's truthful “no.” The practical constraints establish the book's moral economy quickly, even though the opening assumes substantial prior context. | High |
+| Keep reading | 9 | Ch. 24, the paper hidden under Pike's stone; ch. 25, Teague's reasoned refusal; ch. 30, the absent instruments and occupied den. Warnings are neither arbitrary nor fully explained, and each answer sharpens rather than dissipates pressure. | High |
+| Interest / freshness | 9 | Chs. 26–30, the blank official map versus the carter's useful map, the geometrically exact dead zone, and Cael's anomalous fragments. The movement makes measurement, recordkeeping, and local knowledge dramatic. | High |
+| Clarity / flow | 8 | Ch. 27, formation at the boundary, and ch. 30, the ten-cord approach make spatial relations easy to visualize. The chapter 28 survey slightly over-explains its method, while the chs. 25 and 29 placeholders are severe but localized defects. | High |
+| Character attachment | 9 | Ch. 25 council, especially Karis declaring her bias, and ch. 29, Oryn proving her hands fail before choosing to enter. The crew members have differentiated values and take agency-bearing risks. | High |
+| Humor / warmth | 8 | Ch. 23, the carter's grave boot negotiation; ch. 26, Cael rationing his letters; ch. 29, Brom testing whether the box still weighs the same. Affection emerges from habits rather than sentimentality. | High |
+| Action / suspense | 9 | Ch. 29's escalating controlled trials lead directly to ch. 30's timed exploration and late den discovery. The movement earns its length by turning procedural confidence into tactical vulnerability. | High |
+| Progression payoff | 9 | Ch. 23 recovery milestones, ch. 26 Karis's restored Ember, and ch. 29 proof that trained bodies and learned craft remain when Paths do not. Progression is physical, professional, and ethical, not only numerical. | High |
+| Connection | 9 | Chs. 23–30 carry debts, injuries, board rank, the nine held fragments, registry interest, maps, letters, and healer promises across scenes with strong causal linkage. Most plants mature into decisions rather than reminders. | High |
+| Read-aloud quality | 8 | Ch. 24, Pike's explanation of quiet ground, and ch. 30, the final page, have controlled cadence and strong sentence endings. Dense semicolon-linked analysis and unfinished protected-text slots prevent a higher score. | High |
